<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aimoré</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Botocudo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aimoré were Macro-Jê-speaking peoples of the Atlantic forest of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>, Espírito Santo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, later called Botocudos by the Portuguese for their lip and ear plugs. From the mid-16th century their raids devastated the captaincies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilheus">Ilhéus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> Seguro. They were attacked in official wars into the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aimore.jpg" alt="Aimoré" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ferrario, Giulio (1767-1847) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Botocudos-Chief-Brasil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
